		<div style="font-size:13px;display:block;margin-block-end:13px;margin-block-start:13px;margin-inline-end:0px;margin-inline-start:0px;">It's simple: with older kernels, the interface was always considered to be up, so one could just throw anything out of it and tcpdump would get it. Starting from kernel 2.6.36, a change was added that sets the interface "up" only when there is a program connected to the interface, which is effectively the equivalent of a "carrier" or a "wire" for the tun interface. It's no different from what you see with a regular ethernet interface: if the cable is disconnected, you get a "no-carrier" status. With this new feature, it's no longer possible to ping an address belonging to the tun interface subnet and see the packets going out, unless a program has previously been attached to the interface.</div>

		
