<div style="font-size:13px;display:block;margin-block-end:13px;margin-block-start:13px;margin-inline-end:0px;margin-inline-start:0px;">regarding the CRC: it seems it's not part of the frame you get when read()ing the tap descriptor. It's also easy to verify with a simple test (eg, ping): a standard ping under Linux gives you 98 bytes of data from the tap descriptor; of these, 84 are the IP+ICMP stuff, and you have 14 bytes left which must be the ethernet data. Since the source and destination MAC are 6 bytes each, the type/length is another 2 bytes, 6+6+2=14 and thus there's nothing else.</div>
<div style="font-size:13px;display:block;margin-block-end:13px;margin-block-start:13px;margin-inline-end:0px;margin-inline-start:0px;"> Regarding traffic: that tap interface doesn't attach to any NIC; it is an interface on its own. You attach to it to read its traffic. What you get when reading the tap interface is the traffic that the kernel decides has to be routed through the tap interface. So, you have to manage this at the kernel/routing level (iptables, iproute2). You want that the kernel only send the traffic you're interested in to the tap interface.</div>

		
