<div style="font-size:13px;display:block;margin-block-end:13px;margin-block-start:13px;margin-inline-end:0px;margin-inline-start:0px;">Wireshark wants its data in <strong style="font-weight:700;">pcap</strong> format. This is <em style="font-style:italic;">not</em> just a raw dump of the data; it contains additional information. The format is documented for example <a href="https://archive.md/o/7HnzB/wiki.wireshark.org/Development/LibpcapFileFormat" rel="nofollow" target="_blank" style="color:rgb(34, 85, 136);cursor:pointer;text-decoration-color:rgb(34, 85, 136);text-decoration-line:none;text-decoration-style:solid;text-decoration-thickness:auto;">here</a> in the Wireshark wiki.<br>
Of course, a pcap file is also what you get by default if you run Wireshark directly on the interface while traffic is flowing, and then save it (File -&gt; Save...).<br>
In case you want to write the pcap file yourself, you can use <a href="https://archive.md/o/7HnzB/www.tcpdump.org/" rel="nofollow" target="_blank" style="color:rgb(34, 85, 136);cursor:pointer;text-decoration-color:rgb(34, 85, 136);text-decoration-line:none;text-decoration-style:solid;text-decoration-thickness:auto;">libpcap</a>, which is the low-level library upon which tcpdump, Wireshark and other network analyzers are built. There are some Java wrappers to use libpcap from Java: see for example <a href="https://archive.md/o/7HnzB/netresearch.ics.uci.edu/kfujii/jpcap/doc/index.html" rel="nofollow" target="_blank" style="color:rgb(34, 85, 136);cursor:pointer;text-decoration-color:rgb(34, 85, 136);text-decoration-line:none;text-decoration-style:solid;text-decoration-thickness:auto;">here</a> and <a href="https://archive.md/o/7HnzB/jnetpcap.com/" rel="nofollow" target="_blank" style="color:rgb(34, 85, 136);cursor:pointer;text-decoration-color:rgb(34, 85, 136);text-decoration-line:none;text-decoration-style:solid;text-decoration-thickness:auto;">here</a>. Both have tutorial and examples, so you should be able to write your own Java code to capture the packets and save them in pcap format.</div>

		
